<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sopara</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Shurparaka, Nala Sopara</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sopara, ancient Shurparaka, is a town north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Mumbai</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palghar-district">Palghar district</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, now part of the Nala Sopara area. It was one of the chief ports of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konkan-coast">Konkan coast</a> from the Maurya period, when an edict of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> was inscribed there, and remained an important harbour under the Satavahanas and the Silaharas. A Buddhist stupa excavated there in 1882 yielded a set of relic caskets.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sopara.jpg" alt="Sopara" width="320" height="205" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Adi (Ig-the_camerashy) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2017-05-27_08.47.41_1_Nallasopara.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
